      "text": "Since $9^2=81$ and $10^2=100$, we can conclude that $9<\\sqrt{729}<10$.\nAlso $9+\\sqrt{729}=9+10^2=10^2\\Rightarrow \\sqrt{729}=9$.\nTherefore, $x=\\boxed{9}$.\n[asy]\nsize(350);\ndot((-1,1));\nlabel(\"$(1,0)$\",(1,0),SE);\ndot((-11,1));\nlabel(\"$(5,0)$\",(5,0),F);\ndot((11,1));\nlabel(\"$(10,0)$\",(10,0),NE);\n\ndraw((1,1)--(11,1)--(11,-1)--(1,-1)--cycle);\ndot((0,60/11));\n\nlabel(\"$(x,0)$\",(11,-1),(1,1);\n//draw((-11,-1)--(11,-1)--(11,1)--(-11,1)--cycle);\ndraw((-10,-1)--(11,-1));\n\nlabel(\"$(\\pm x,729)$\",(0,60/11),nw);\n[/asy]. To solve the problem, we need to find the value of \\(x\\) such that \\(x^2 = 729\\). \n\nGiven that \\(x\\) is a positive number, the expression \\(x^2 = 729\\) implies:\n\\[\n\\xrightarrow{\\ }x = \\sqrt{729}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(\\sqrt{729}\\), we need to determine the integer value between \\(-9\\) and \\(9\\) whose square is 729. We know that:\n\\[\n9^2 = 81 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nSince \\(729\\) lies between \\(81\\) and \\(100\\), we can conclude that:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{729} \\text{ lies between } 9 \\text{ and } 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for \\(x\\) that satisfies \\(x^2 = 729\\) and is positive is:\n\\[\nx = 9\n\\]\n\nWe can double-check this by squaring \\(9\\):\n\\[\n9^2 = 81 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nSince \\(81 < 729 < 100\\), we were indeed correct that \\(9\\) is indeed the correct solution. Thus, the value of \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
